// Class 12 Physics practice bank, part 2: chapters 8-14 (Electromagnetic Waves to Semiconductor Electronics).
StudyData.addPractice(12, "physics", {
  "electromagnetic-waves": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following electromagnetic radiations has the longest wavelength?", options: ["X-rays", "Infrared", "Radio waves", "Ultraviolet"], answer: 2, explain: "In order of decreasing wavelength: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays. Radio waves are the longest.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The current in the gap between the plates of a charging parallel-plate capacitor is:", options: ["conduction current", "displacement current", "eddy current", "zero"], answer: 1, explain: "No charges cross the gap. The changing electric flux there produces the displacement current i_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt, which equals the conduction current in the wires.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In an electromagnetic wave, the direction of propagation is along:", options: ["B × E", "E", "B", "E × B"], answer: 3, explain: "E, B and the direction of travel are mutually perpendicular, and the wave travels along E × B.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum is given by:", options: ["√(μ₀ε₀)", "μ₀ε₀", "1/√(μ₀ε₀)", "1/(μ₀ε₀)"], answer: 2, explain: "Maxwell showed c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀) ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The greenhouse effect keeps the Earth warm mainly by trapping:", options: ["infrared radiation", "ultraviolet radiation", "X-rays", "microwaves"], answer: 0, explain: "Gases like CO₂ and water vapour absorb the infrared radiation emitted by the Earth's surface and re-radiate it, warming the planet.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The electric field amplitude of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is 150 V m⁻¹. The amplitude of its magnetic field is:", options: ["4.5 × 10¹⁰ T", "5 × 10⁻⁷ T", "5 × 10⁻⁹ T", "2 × 10⁶ T"], answer: 1, explain: "B₀ = E₀/c = 150 / (3 × 10⁸) = 5 × 10⁻⁷ T.", level: 2 },
      { q: "An FM radio station broadcasts at 100 MHz. The wavelength of its waves in air is about:", options: ["0.3 m", "3 m", "30 m", "300 m"], answer: 1, explain: "λ = c/ν = 3 × 10⁸ / (100 × 10⁶) = 3 m.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Food containing water gets heated quickly in a microwave oven.\nReason (R): The frequency of the microwaves matches the resonant frequency of water molecules, so the molecules absorb energy strongly.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Water molecules absorb microwaves at resonance, gain energy and heat the food. R correctly explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A welder notices that the arc gives off bright light, heat and an invisible radiation that can damage the eyes and skin. The special glass in the welder's goggles is mainly meant to cut off:", options: ["radio waves", "microwaves", "gamma rays", "ultraviolet radiation"], answer: 3, explain: "A very hot arc emits strong ultraviolet radiation, which damages tissue. Welding goggles use special glass to absorb it.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The charge on the plates of a capacitor is increasing at the rate of 2 μC s⁻¹. The displacement current between the plates is:", options: ["zero", "2 μA", "4 μA", "1 μA"], answer: 1, explain: "i_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt = dq/dt for a parallel-plate capacitor, so i_d equals the rate of change of charge: 2 μC s⁻¹ = 2 μA.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A pulse of electromagnetic radiation carrying 6 J of energy is completely absorbed by a black surface. The momentum delivered to the surface is:", options: ["1.8 × 10⁹ kg m s⁻¹", "2 × 10⁻⁹ kg m s⁻¹", "2 × 10⁻⁸ kg m s⁻¹", "4 × 10⁻⁸ kg m s⁻¹"], answer: 2, explain: "On complete absorption p = U/c = 6 / (3 × 10⁸) = 2 × 10⁻⁸ kg m s⁻¹. The 4 × 10⁻⁸ value would apply to total reflection (2U/c).", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Electromagnetic waves can travel through a vacuum.\nReason (R): Electromagnetic waves are transverse in nature.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both are true. The wave needs no medium because a changing E produces B and a changing B produces E. Being transverse does not explain this: sound waves in air are not transverse, but transverse mechanical waves still need a medium.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 15 MHz travels in vacuum and has an electric field amplitude of 90 V m⁻¹. Its wavelength and magnetic field amplitude are:", options: ["5 m and 3 × 10⁻⁷ T", "20 m and 3 × 10⁻⁷ T", "20 m and 3 × 10⁻⁹ T", "0.05 m and 2.7 × 10¹⁰ T"], answer: 1, explain: "λ = c/ν = 3 × 10⁸ / (15 × 10⁶) = 20 m. B₀ = E₀/c = 90 / (3 × 10⁸) = 3 × 10⁻⁷ T.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A circular-plate capacitor of plate radius R is being charged by a steady current I. A student wants to know the magnetic field at a point between the plates, at a distance r < R from the axis.\nThe magnetic field there is:", options: ["zero, because no conduction current flows between the plates", "μ₀I/(2πr), as for a long straight wire", "non-zero only when the plates are very close together", "non-zero, produced by the displacement current, and increasing with r for r < R"], answer: 3, explain: "A changing electric flux in the gap acts like a current. Applying the Ampere–Maxwell law to a circle of radius r < R gives B (2πr) = μ₀I r²/R², so B = μ₀Ir/(2πR²), which grows with r.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define displacement current. Write its expression.", a: "Displacement current is the current that arises from a time-varying electric flux (not from the flow of charge). Expression: i_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt, where Φ_E is the electric flux.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why are electromagnetic waves not deflected by electric or magnetic fields?", a: "Electromagnetic waves carry no electric charge, so an external electric or magnetic field exerts no force (qE or qv × B) on them and they are not deflected.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "An electromagnetic wave has a wavelength of 5 m in vacuum and an electric field amplitude of 30 V m⁻¹. Find its frequency and the amplitude of its magnetic field.", a: "Frequency ν = c/λ = (3 × 10⁸)/5 = 6 × 10⁷ Hz = 60 MHz.\nB₀ = E₀/c = 30/(3 × 10⁸) = 1 × 10⁻⁷ T.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the electromagnetic waves produced (a) in nuclear reactions and (b) when high-energy electrons strike a metal target. Give one use of each.", a: "(a) Gamma rays: used to destroy cancer cells in medicine.\n(b) X-rays: used in medical diagnosis (imaging bones) and in the treatment of some cancers.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State the Ampere–Maxwell law. Explain the meaning of each term and what the added term shows about electric and magnetic fields.", a: "Ampere–Maxwell law: ∮B · dl = μ₀(i_c + i_d) = μ₀i_c + μ₀ε₀ dΦ_E/dt.\n(i) ∮B · dl is the line integral of the magnetic field around a closed loop; i_c is the conduction current through the surface bounded by the loop.\n(ii) i_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt is the displacement current due to a changing electric flux; it was added by Maxwell so that the total current is continuous.\n(iii) The added term shows that a changing electric field produces a magnetic field, the counterpart of Faraday's law (a changing magnetic field produces an electric field).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write the following in order of increasing frequency: X-rays, microwaves, ultraviolet, gamma rays, infrared, radio waves. Give one use each of microwaves and ultraviolet radiation.", a: "Increasing frequency: radio waves < microwaves < infrared < ultraviolet < X-rays < gamma rays.\nMicrowaves: used in radar and microwave ovens.\nUltraviolet: used to kill germs in water purifiers (also in LASIK eye surgery).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The plates of a parallel-plate capacitor of capacitance 50 pF are being charged so that the potential difference across it rises at 4 × 10⁶ V s⁻¹. Find the displacement current between the plates.", a: "For plates of area A and separation d: E = V/d, so Φ_E = EA = VA/d.\ni_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt = (ε₀A/d)(dV/dt) = C (dV/dt).\ni_d = 50 × 10⁻¹² × 4 × 10⁶ = 2 × 10⁻⁴ A = 200 μA.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A capacitor is being charged. (a) Explain why Ampere's circuital law gives conflicting results for two surfaces bounded by the same loop around a connecting wire. (b) Show that the displacement current between the plates equals the conduction current in the wires. (c) State the resulting modified law.", a: "(a) Surface 1 is a flat disc cut by the wire: conduction current i_c passes through it, so ∮B · dl = μ₀i_c. Surface 2 is a bowl-shaped surface passing through the gap between the plates: no current passes through it, so ∮B · dl = 0. The same loop cannot give two values.\n(b) Let the plates have area A and charge q. The field in the gap is E = q/(ε₀A), so Φ_E = EA = q/ε₀. Then i_d = ε₀ dΦ_E/dt = dq/dt = i_c.\n(c) Including i_d gives ∮B · dl = μ₀(i_c + i_d), so both surfaces now give μ₀i_c and the contradiction vanishes.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "(a) List the important properties of electromagnetic waves. (b) Using c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀), calculate c given μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m A⁻¹ and ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C² N⁻¹ m⁻².", a: "(a) Properties:\n(i) They are produced by accelerated (oscillating) charges.\n(ii) They are transverse: E, B and the direction of travel are mutually perpendicular, and E and B are in phase.\n(iii) They need no medium; in vacuum they travel at c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀).\n(iv) E₀/B₀ = c.\n(v) They carry energy and momentum, so they exert radiation pressure.\n(b) μ₀ε₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 8.85 × 10⁻¹² = 1.112 × 10⁻¹⁷ s² m⁻²\n√(μ₀ε₀) = 3.335 × 10⁻⁹ s m⁻¹\nc = 1/(3.335 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A domestic microwave oven works at a frequency of 2.45 GHz. The waves are absorbed by the water in food, which heats up, while dry plastic or glass plates stay cool. A local FM radio station broadcasts at 98 MHz.\n(i) Find the wavelength of the microwaves in the oven.\n(ii) Why does the food heat up but a dry glass plate does not?\n(iii) How many times is the oven frequency greater than that of the FM station?", a: "(i) λ = c/ν = (3 × 10⁸)/(2.45 × 10⁹) ≈ 0.12 m (about 12 cm).\n(ii) The oven frequency matches the resonant frequency of water molecules, which absorb the energy and heat the food. Dry glass has no such resonance and absorbs very little.\n(iii) Ratio = 2.45 × 10⁹ / 98 × 10⁶ = 25.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "ray-optics-and-optical-instruments": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "For a real object, a convex mirror always forms an image that is:", options: ["real and magnified", "real and inverted", "virtual, erect and diminished", "virtual, erect and magnified"], answer: 2, explain: "A convex mirror diverges rays, so for any real object the image is virtual, erect, diminished and lies behind the mirror between P and F.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the Cartesian sign convention, the focal length of a concave mirror is:", options: ["positive", "negative", "zero", "positive or negative depending on the object distance"], answer: 1, explain: "Distances are measured from the pole, with the direction of incident light as positive. The focus of a concave mirror lies in front of it, so f is negative.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The image formed by the objective of a compound microscope is:", options: ["real, inverted and magnified", "virtual, erect and magnified", "real, erect and diminished", "virtual, inverted and diminished"], answer: 0, explain: "The object lies just beyond the focus of the objective, so it forms a real, inverted, magnified image, which the eyepiece then magnifies further.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Total internal reflection can take place when light travels:", options: ["from a rarer to a denser medium at any angle", "from a rarer to a denser medium at a large angle", "from a denser to a rarer medium at an angle less than the critical angle", "from a denser to a rarer medium at an angle greater than the critical angle"], answer: 3, explain: "Both conditions are needed: light must go from a denser to a rarer medium, and the angle of incidence must exceed the critical angle.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The critical angle for light going from a medium of refractive index 2 into air is:", options: ["60°", "45°", "30°", "90°"], answer: 2, explain: "sin i_c = 1/n = 1/2, so i_c = 30°.", level: 1 },

      { q: "An object is placed 30 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. The image is formed:", options: ["30 cm in front of the mirror, real, inverted and of the same size", "10 cm in front of the mirror, real and diminished", "30 cm behind the mirror, virtual and of the same size", "60 cm in front of the mirror, real and magnified"], answer: 0, explain: "Here u = −30 cm and f = −15 cm. 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = −1/15 + 1/30 = −1/30, so v = −30 cm. m = −v/u = −1: real, inverted, same size. The object is at the centre of curvature.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A biconvex lens made of glass of refractive index 1.5 has both radii of curvature equal to 20 cm. Its focal length in air is:", options: ["10 cm", "40 cm", "20 cm", "30 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂) = 0.5 × (1/20 + 1/20) = 0.05 cm⁻¹, so f = 20 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A glass prism of angle 60° has refractive index √3. The angle of minimum deviation is:", options: ["30°", "45°", "90°", "60°"], answer: 3, explain: "n = sin[(A + D)/2]/sin(A/2), so sin[(60° + D)/2] = √3 × sin 30° = √3/2. Hence (60° + D)/2 = 60°, giving D = 60°.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A swimming pool appears shallower than its actual depth when viewed from above.\nReason (R): Light rays bend away from the normal when they go from water into air.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Rays from the bottom bend away from the normal on leaving water, so they appear to come from a point higher up. Apparent depth = real depth/n.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A jeweller uses a convex lens of focal length 5 cm as a magnifying glass and views a gem so that the image forms at the near point (D = 25 cm).\nThe magnifying power of the lens is:", options: ["5", "6", "4", "30"], answer: 1, explain: "With the image at the near point, m = 1 + D/f = 1 + 25/5 = 6. The value D/f = 5 applies when the image is at infinity.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A compound microscope has an objective of focal length 1 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. The distance between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece is 20 cm. The magnification with the final image at infinity is (D = 25 cm):", options: ["25", "500", "120", "100"], answer: 3, explain: "m = (L/f_o)(D/f_e) = (20/1) × (25/5) = 20 × 5 = 100.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A refracting telescope in normal adjustment has an angular magnification of 40 and a tube length of 102.5 cm. The focal lengths of the objective and eyepiece are respectively:", options: ["102.5 cm and 2.5 cm", "95 cm and 2.5 cm", "100 cm and 2.5 cm", "40 cm and 1 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "m = f_o/f_e = 40 and f_o + f_e = 102.5 cm. So 41 f_e = 102.5 cm, f_e = 2.5 cm and f_o = 100 cm.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Light going from water (n = 4/3) to air can undergo total internal reflection at an angle of incidence of 50°.\nReason (R): Total internal reflection can occur when light goes from air into water at a large angle of incidence.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "The critical angle for water is sin⁻¹(3/4) ≈ 48.6°, and 50° is greater, so A is true. R is false: TIR needs light to travel from a denser to a rarer medium, never from air into water.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: An optician places a convex lens of power +5 D in contact with a concave lens of focal length 50 cm to make a corrective lens.\nThe power and focal length of the combination are:", options: ["+7 D and 14.3 cm", "+3 D and 33.3 cm", "−3 D and 33.3 cm", "+2.5 D and 40 cm"], answer: 1, explain: "Power of the concave lens = 1/(−0.5 m) = −2 D. Total power = 5 − 2 = +3 D, so f = 1/3 m ≈ 33.3 cm (converging).", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the power of a lens. Write its SI unit.", a: "The power of a lens is the reciprocal of its focal length in metres, P = 1/f. It measures the ability of the lens to converge or diverge light. SI unit: dioptre (D), 1 D = 1 m⁻¹.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why does a diamond sparkle?", a: "Diamond has a very high refractive index (about 2.4), so its critical angle is very small (about 24°). Light entering it undergoes repeated total internal reflections before emerging, which makes it sparkle.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why should the objective of a refracting telescope have a large focal length and a large aperture?", a: "A large focal length gives a large magnification, m = f_o/f_e. A large aperture collects more light, so faint objects look brighter, and it gives better resolving power.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "An object 5 cm high is placed 20 cm from a convex lens of focal length 15 cm. Find the position, nature and size of the image.", a: "u = −20 cm, f = +15 cm. 1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/15 − 1/20 = 1/60, so v = +60 cm (on the other side of the lens).\nm = v/u = 60/(−20) = −3.\nImage height = 3 × 5 = 15 cm. The image is real, inverted and magnified.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Derive the relation n = sin[(A + D)/2] / sin(A/2) for a prism at the position of minimum deviation.", a: "(i) For a prism, A = r₁ + r₂ and the deviation is δ = i + e − A.\n(ii) At minimum deviation i = e and r₁ = r₂ = r. So A = 2r, i.e. r = A/2, and D = 2i − A, i.e. i = (A + D)/2.\n(iii) By Snell's law n = sin i / sin r = sin[(A + D)/2] / sin(A/2).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Two thin lenses of focal lengths +20 cm and −30 cm are placed in contact. Find the focal length and power of the combination and state whether it is converging or diverging.", a: "1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂ = 1/20 − 1/30 = (3 − 2)/60 = 1/60.\nf = +60 cm = 0.6 m.\nPower P = 1/0.6 ≈ +1.67 D. Since f is positive, the combination is converging.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State the conditions for total internal reflection. Calculate the critical angle for light going from glass of refractive index 1.5 to air.", a: "Conditions: (i) light must travel from a denser to a rarer medium; (ii) the angle of incidence must be greater than the critical angle.\nsin i_c = 1/n = 1/1.5 = 2/3, so i_c = sin⁻¹(0.667) ≈ 41.8° (about 42°).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive the relation n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R for refraction at a convex spherical surface separating two media of refractive indices n₁ and n₂ (paraxial rays).", a: "A ray from a point object O meets the surface at N, with centre C, pole P and image I. Let NM be the perpendicular from N to the axis; for paraxial rays M ≈ P.\n(i) In triangle NOC, the exterior angle gives i = α + γ. In triangle NCI, γ = r + β, so r = γ − β.\n(ii) For small angles Snell's law gives n₁i = n₂r, so n₁(α + γ) = n₂(γ − β).\n(iii) Angles are small, so α ≈ NM/MO, β ≈ NM/MI, γ ≈ NM/MC.\n(iv) With the Cartesian convention MO = −u, MI = +v, MC = +R: n₁(1/(−u) + 1/R) = n₂(1/R − 1/v).\n(v) Rearranging: n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "With the help of a ray diagram, derive an expression for the magnification of a compound microscope when the final image is formed at infinity.", a: "Diagram: the object AB lies just beyond the focus of the objective (focal length f_o); the objective forms a real, inverted, magnified image A′B′ just inside the focal point of the eyepiece (focal length f_e); the eyepiece acts as a simple microscope and gives a final virtual image at infinity.\n(i) Magnification of the objective: m_o = height of A′B′ / height of AB = v_o/|u_o|. Since the object is close to the focus of the objective and the image is formed near the focus of the eyepiece, v_o ≈ L (the tube length), and u_o ≈ f_o, so m_o ≈ L/f_o.\n(ii) Final image at infinity means A′B′ is at the focus of the eyepiece, so m_e = D/f_e.\n(iii) Total magnification m = m_o × m_e = (L/f_o)(D/f_e), where D = 25 cm.\n(iv) It is large when f_o and f_e are small and L is large. The final image is inverted.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A biconvex lens made of glass of refractive index 1.5 has both faces of radius of curvature 30 cm. A student first uses it in air and then immerses it in a transparent liquid of refractive index 1.5.\n(i) Find the focal length of the lens in air.\n(ii) Find its power in air.\n(iii) What happens to the lens when it is in the liquid?", a: "(i) 1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂) = 0.5 × (1/30 + 1/30) = 1/30, so f = 30 cm.\n(ii) P = 1/f = 1/0.30 ≈ +3.33 D.\n(iii) In the liquid the relative refractive index is 1.5/1.5 = 1, so 1/f = 0 and f becomes infinite. The lens does not bend light (power zero) and behaves like a plain glass plate; it is practically invisible in the liquid.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "wave-optics": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The shape of the wavefront from a point source in a homogeneous medium is:", options: ["plane", "spherical", "cylindrical", "conical"], answer: 1, explain: "Waves spread equally in all directions from a point source, so the points in the same phase lie on a sphere.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When light passes from air into glass, which of its quantities remains unchanged?", options: ["wavelength", "speed", "frequency", "intensity"], answer: 2, explain: "The frequency is fixed by the source. The speed falls to c/n and the wavelength to λ/n.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A sustained interference pattern is obtained only from sources that are:", options: ["coherent", "of different frequencies", "of random phase", "of different colours"], answer: 0, explain: "Coherent sources have the same frequency and a constant phase difference, so the pattern stays steady in time.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Unpolarised light of intensity I₀ passes through an ideal polaroid. The intensity of the emerging light is:", options: ["I₀", "I₀/4", "zero", "I₀/2"], answer: 3, explain: "A polaroid passes only the component of E along its axis; averaged over all directions this is half the intensity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In Young's double-slit experiment the fringe width increases when:", options: ["the slit separation is increased", "the wavelength is decreased", "the screen is moved farther away", "the screen is moved closer"], answer: 2, explain: "β = λD/d, so β increases with larger λ or D and decreases with larger d.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In Young's experiment the slits are 0.3 mm apart and the screen is 1.5 m from them. For light of wavelength 600 nm, the fringe width is:", options: ["3 mm", "0.3 mm", "6 mm", "1.5 mm"], answer: 0, explain: "β = λD/d = 600 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.5 / (0.3 × 10⁻³) = 3 × 10⁻³ m = 3 mm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Plane polarised light of intensity I₀ falls on a polaroid whose axis makes 60° with the plane of polarisation. The transmitted intensity is:", options: ["I₀/2", "I₀/4", "3I₀/4", "I₀"], answer: 1, explain: "By Malus's law I = I₀ cos²60° = I₀ × (1/2)² = I₀/4.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Light of wavelength 500 nm falls on a single slit of width 0.2 mm. The width of the central maximum on a screen 1.2 m away is:", options: ["1.2 mm", "3 mm", "12 mm", "6 mm"], answer: 3, explain: "Width = 2λD/a = 2 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.2 / (2 × 10⁻⁴) = 6 × 10⁻³ m = 6 mm.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Two independent sodium lamps cannot produce a steady interference pattern.\nReason (R): The phase difference between light from two independent sources changes randomly and rapidly, so they are not coherent.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Atoms emit light in random, short bursts, so two separate lamps have no fixed phase relation. The fringes shift constantly and average out, so R correctly explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A student views a filament bulb through a polaroid sheet and rotates the sheet slowly through 360°. The brightness stays the same throughout but is lower than when the bulb is seen without the polaroid.\nThis shows that:", options: ["the light is plane polarised and the polaroid is faulty", "light is a longitudinal wave", "the light is unpolarised and the polaroid transmits half of its intensity", "the light is partially polarised along the axis"], answer: 2, explain: "For unpolarised light every direction of vibration is equally likely, so the transmitted intensity (I₀/2) does not depend on the orientation of the polaroid.", level: 2 },

      { q: "In a double-slit experiment with λ = 500 nm, d = 0.5 mm and D = 1 m, the distance of the third dark fringe from the central bright fringe is:", options: ["1.5 mm", "3.0 mm", "3.5 mm", "2.5 mm"], answer: 3, explain: "β = λD/d = 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 1 / (0.5 × 10⁻³) = 1 mm. The first dark fringe is at β/2, the second at 3β/2 and the third at 5β/2 = 2.5 mm.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two coherent sources have intensities I and 4I. The ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern is:", options: ["5 : 3", "9 : 1", "3 : 1", "25 : 9"], answer: 1, explain: "I_max = (√I + √(4I))² = (3√I)² = 9I and I_min = (√(4I) − √I)² = I. Ratio = 9 : 1.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In single-slit diffraction, the central maximum becomes narrower when the slit is made wider.\nReason (R): The width of the central maximum depends only on the wavelength of the light and not on the slit width.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Angular width = 2λ/a, so a wider slit gives a narrower central maximum (A is true). R is false because the width also depends on a.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a lab, unpolarised light of intensity 80 W m⁻² passes through a polaroid P₁ and then through a second polaroid P₂ whose axis makes 60° with that of P₁.\nThe intensity of light emerging from P₂ is:", options: ["20 W m⁻²", "10 W m⁻²", "30 W m⁻²", "40 W m⁻²"], answer: 1, explain: "After P₁: 80/2 = 40 W m⁻². After P₂: 40 cos²60° = 40 × 1/4 = 10 W m⁻².", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Huygens' principle.", a: "Every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets that spread out in the forward direction with the speed of the wave. The new wavefront at a later time is the forward envelope (common tangent) of these secondary wavelets.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why can sound waves in air not be polarised, while light waves can?", a: "Polarisation requires vibrations perpendicular to the direction of travel. Light is a transverse wave, so it can be polarised; sound waves in air are longitudinal, so they cannot.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Light of wavelength 600 nm in air enters glass of refractive index 1.5. Find its wavelength and speed in glass. Does its frequency change?", a: "λ′ = λ/n = 600/1.5 = 400 nm.\nv = c/n = (3 × 10⁸)/1.5 = 2 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.\nThe frequency does not change; it is determined by the source.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What changes are seen in the fringe pattern of Young's experiment if monochromatic light is replaced by white light?", a: "The central fringe remains white, since all colours are bright there. On both sides there are a few coloured fringes, because β = λD/d differs for each colour. In each fringe violet lies nearest the centre and red farthest. Beyond a few fringes the colours overlap and the pattern fades into uniform illumination.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In Young's double-slit experiment, derive an expression for the fringe width.", a: "Let the slits S₁, S₂ be d apart, with the screen at distance D (D ≫ d). For a point P at distance x from the centre, the path difference is S₂P − S₁P ≈ xd/D.\nBright fringes: xd/D = nλ, so x_n = nλD/d.\nFringe width = separation of successive bright (or dark) fringes: β = x_{n+1} − x_n = λD/d.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Two coherent sources have intensities in the ratio 16 : 1. Find the ratio of the maximum to the minimum intensity in the interference pattern.", a: "I₁ : I₂ = 16 : 1, so √I₁ : √I₂ = 4 : 1.\nI_max = (√I₁ + √I₂)² ∝ (4 + 1)² = 25.\nI_min = (√I₁ − √I₂)² ∝ (4 − 1)² = 9.\nI_max : I_min = 25 : 9.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Unpolarised light of intensity I₀ passes through three polaroids P₁, P₂ and P₃ in turn. The axis of P₂ makes 45° with P₁ and the axis of P₃ makes 90° with P₁. Find the final intensity.", a: "After P₁: I₁ = I₀/2.\nAfter P₂ (45° to P₁): I₂ = I₁ cos²45° = (I₀/2)(1/2) = I₀/4.\nP₃ is at 90° − 45° = 45° to P₂: I₃ = I₂ cos²45° = (I₀/4)(1/2) = I₀/8.\nFinal intensity = I₀/8.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using Huygens' principle, derive Snell's law of refraction for a plane wavefront incident on a plane surface separating two media in which the speeds of light are v₁ and v₂.", a: "(i) Let a plane wavefront AB in medium 1 (speed v₁) fall on the surface PP′; the angle between AB and the surface equals the angle of incidence i.\n(ii) While point B of the wavefront travels to C on the surface in time τ, BC = v₁τ. In the same time the secondary wavelet from A in medium 2 spreads to a distance AE = v₂τ.\n(iii) The refracted wavefront is the tangent CE from C to this wavelet; the angle between CE and the surface equals the angle of refraction r.\n(iv) In right triangle ABC, sin i = BC/AC = v₁τ/AC. In right triangle AEC, sin r = AE/AC = v₂τ/AC.\n(v) Dividing: sin i / sin r = v₁/v₂ = constant (= n₂/n₁), which is Snell's law.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "In Young's experiment the slits are 0.4 mm apart and the screen is 1.2 m away. The wavelength of light is 500 nm. Find (a) the fringe width, (b) the distance of the 4th bright fringe from the centre, (c) the distance of the 3rd dark fringe from the centre, (d) the fringe width if the whole apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.25.", a: "(a) β = λD/d = 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 1.2 / (0.4 × 10⁻³) = 1.5 × 10⁻³ m = 1.5 mm.\n(b) x₄ = 4β = 6 mm.\n(c) Third dark fringe: x = (2 + ½)β = 2.5 × 1.5 = 3.75 mm.\n(d) In the liquid λ′ = λ/n = 500/1.25 = 400 nm, so β′ = β/n = 1.5/1.25 = 1.2 mm.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A student uses a laser of wavelength 650 nm to study diffraction at a single slit of width 0.13 mm. The pattern is observed on a screen 2.0 m away.\n(i) Find the angular width of the central maximum.\n(ii) Find the linear width of the central maximum on the screen.\n(iii) What happens to the width of the central maximum if the slit is made half as wide?", a: "(i) Angular width = 2λ/a = 2 × 650 × 10⁻⁹ / (0.13 × 10⁻³) = 1.0 × 10⁻² rad.\n(ii) Linear width = 2λD/a = (1.0 × 10⁻²) × 2.0 = 2 × 10⁻² m = 2 cm.\n(iii) Width is inversely proportional to a, so halving the slit width doubles the central maximum to 4 cm.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "dual-nature-of-radiation-and-matter": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The photoelectric effect gives evidence for:", options: ["the wave nature of light", "the longitudinal nature of light", "the particle (photon) nature of light", "the polarisation of light"], answer: 2, explain: "Emission occurs only when single photons of sufficient energy hν are absorbed by electrons, which shows light behaves as particles in its interaction with matter.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For a given photosensitive metal, the stopping potential depends on:", options: ["the intensity of the incident light", "the frequency of the incident light", "the area of the cathode", "the distance of the source"], answer: 1, explain: "eV₀ = K_max = hν − φ₀, so V₀ depends on the frequency (and the metal) but not on the intensity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a graph of stopping potential V₀ against frequency ν of the incident light, the slope of the straight line is:", options: ["h/e", "h", "e/h", "hν₀"], answer: 0, explain: "V₀ = (h/e)ν − φ₀/e, a straight line whose slope is h/e, the same for all metals.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of momentum p is:", options: ["hp", "p/h", "h/p²", "h/p"], answer: 3, explain: "de Broglie's relation is λ = h/p = h/(mv).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The rest mass of a photon is:", options: ["h/c", "infinite", "zero", "1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg"], answer: 2, explain: "A photon always travels at c and has zero rest mass; its energy is hν and its momentum is hν/c.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The energy of a photon of wavelength 620 nm is (take hc = 1240 eV nm):", options: ["2 eV", "1 eV", "3 eV", "0.5 eV"], answer: 0, explain: "E = hc/λ = 1240 eV nm / 620 nm = 2 eV.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on a metal of work function 2.5 eV. The stopping potential is (hc = 1240 eV nm):", options: ["3.1 V", "5.6 V", "0.25 V", "0.6 V"], answer: 3, explain: "E = 1240/400 = 3.1 eV. K_max = 3.1 − 2.5 = 0.6 eV, so V₀ = 0.6 V.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 150 V is about:", options: ["0.01 nm", "0.1 nm", "1.0 nm", "0.5 nm"], answer: 1, explain: "λ = 1.227/√V nm = 1.227/√150 = 1.227/12.25 ≈ 0.10 nm.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): When the intensity of light of fixed frequency (above threshold) is increased, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons increases.\nReason (R): A more intense beam contains a larger number of photons per second.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "K_max = hν − φ₀ depends only on frequency, so A is false. R is true: higher intensity means more photons, hence more photoelectrons and a larger photocurrent, but not more energetic ones.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: The light-sensing switch of a street lamp uses a photocell whose cathode has a threshold wavelength of 560 nm. A student shines four sources on it in turn: a red LED (700 nm), an orange LED (620 nm), a green LED (520 nm) and a yellow LED (580 nm).\nPhotoelectric emission takes place with:", options: ["the red LED only", "the red and orange LEDs", "the green LED only", "the yellow and green LEDs"], answer: 2, explain: "Emission needs λ < threshold wavelength (560 nm). Only 520 nm satisfies this; 580, 620 and 700 nm are longer than the threshold.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Photons of wavelength 248 nm fall on a metal and the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is 2 eV. The threshold wavelength of the metal is about (hc = 1240 eV nm):", options: ["620 nm", "413 nm", "248 nm", "496 nm"], answer: 1, explain: "E = 1240/248 = 5 eV. φ₀ = 5 − 2 = 3 eV. λ₀ = 1240/3 ≈ 413 nm.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A proton and an α-particle are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelengths λ_p : λ_α is:", options: ["2 : 1", "√2 : 1", "4 : 1", "2√2 : 1"], answer: 3, explain: "λ = h/√(2mqV). With m_α = 4m_p and q_α = 2q_p: λ_p/λ_α = √(m_αq_α/(m_pq_p)) = √(4 × 2) = 2√2.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A photon and an electron of the same wavelength have the same momentum.\nReason (R): A photon has zero rest mass.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "The momentum of any particle or photon is p = h/λ, so equal wavelengths mean equal momenta (A true). R is also true, but it does not explain A: the momentum relation holds irrespective of rest mass. Their energies differ.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: An electron microscope accelerates electrons through 100 V. A student compares the de Broglie wavelength of these electrons with the 500 nm wavelength of green light used in an optical microscope.\nThe electron wavelength is smaller than that of green light by a factor of about:", options: ["4000", "40", "400", "1"], answer: 0, explain: "λ_e = 1.227/√100 = 0.1227 nm. 500 nm / 0.1227 nm ≈ 4000. A smaller wavelength gives better resolution, which is why electron microscopes see finer detail.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define work function of a metal.", a: "The work function is the minimum energy required to liberate an electron from the surface of the metal. Its unit is the electron volt (eV).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "In a photoelectric experiment the intensity is kept fixed and the frequency of the incident light is increased (above the threshold). What happens to the stopping potential?", a: "The stopping potential increases linearly with frequency, since eV₀ = hν − φ₀.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two observations of the photoelectric effect that the wave theory of light cannot explain.", a: "(i) Threshold frequency: no emission occurs below ν₀, however intense or long the illumination; wave theory predicts emission at any frequency if the intensity is large enough.\n(ii) K_max is independent of intensity and depends only on frequency; wave theory predicts it should increase with intensity. (Also: emission is instantaneous, with no time delay, whereas wave theory predicts a delay.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Calculate the energy (in eV) and the momentum of a photon of wavelength 300 nm. (hc = 1240 eV nm, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)", a: "E = hc/λ = 1240/300 ≈ 4.13 eV.\np = h/λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / (300 × 10⁻⁹) = 2.21 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on a metal surface of work function 2.3 eV. Find (a) the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons in eV, (b) the stopping potential, (c) the threshold wavelength. (hc = 1240 eV nm)", a: "Photon energy E = 1240/400 = 3.1 eV.\n(a) K_max = E − φ₀ = 3.1 − 2.3 = 0.8 eV.\n(b) eV₀ = K_max, so V₀ = 0.8 V.\n(c) λ₀ = hc/φ₀ = 1240/2.3 ≈ 539 nm (about 540 nm).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how Einstein's photoelectric equation accounts for (a) the existence of a threshold frequency, (b) the independence of K_max from intensity, (c) the absence of time lag.", a: "Einstein's equation: K_max = hν − φ₀, with light as photons of energy hν, each absorbed by one electron.\n(a) If hν < φ₀ a photon cannot free an electron, so there is a threshold ν₀ = φ₀/h.\n(b) K_max depends only on ν. Higher intensity means more photons per second and so more electrons (larger current), not more energetic ones.\n(c) An electron absorbs the whole photon in a single event, so emission is instantaneous (about 10⁻⁹ s).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find the de Broglie wavelength of (a) an electron accelerated through 100 V and (b) a ball of mass 0.2 kg moving at 10 m s⁻¹. Comment on the results. (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)", a: "(a) λ = 1.227/√V nm = 1.227/10 = 0.123 nm.\n(b) λ = h/(mv) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / (0.2 × 10) = 3.3 × 10⁻³⁴ m.\nComment: the electron's wavelength is comparable to atomic spacings, so wave effects are observable. The ball's wavelength is far too small to detect, so matter waves are not noticed for everyday objects.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the main experimental observations of the photoelectric effect (photocurrent, stopping potential, threshold frequency, time lag) and state how each depends on the intensity and frequency of the light.", a: "(i) Photocurrent: for a given frequency above threshold, the saturation current is proportional to the intensity of the light.\n(ii) Stopping potential: the retarding potential V₀ that just stops the fastest electrons depends on the frequency (and the metal) but not on the intensity.\n(iii) K_max = eV₀ increases linearly with frequency and is independent of intensity.\n(iv) Threshold frequency: below a certain ν₀, characteristic of the metal, no emission occurs however large the intensity.\n(v) Time lag: emission is instantaneous (within about 10⁻⁹ s or less) even for very dim light.\nThe V₀–ν graph is a straight line with slope h/e and intercept on the frequency axis at ν₀.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "(a) Obtain an expression for the de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated from rest through a potential difference V. (b) Show that it is approximately 1.227/√V nm. (c) Find the wavelength for V = 54 V and state why such electrons can be diffracted by a crystal. (m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s)", a: "(a) The electron gains kinetic energy eV: p²/2m = eV, so p = √(2meV). Then λ = h/p = h/√(2meV).\n(b) 2meV = 2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × V = 2.912 × 10⁻⁴⁹ V, whose square root is 5.40 × 10⁻²⁵ √V. λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ / (5.40 × 10⁻²⁵ √V) = 1.23 × 10⁻⁹/√V m = 1.227/√V nm.\n(c) For V = 54 V: λ = 1.227/√54 = 1.227/7.35 ≈ 0.167 nm.\nThis is comparable to the spacing between atoms in a crystal (about 0.1 nm), so the crystal acts as a diffraction grating for the electrons, which shows their wave nature.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: In a photoelectric experiment a student measured the stopping potential for two frequencies of light: V₀ = 0.60 V at ν = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz and V₀ = 1.43 V at ν = 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz, using the same cathode. (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)\n(i) Find the slope of the V₀–ν line.\n(ii) Use it to find the value of Planck's constant.\n(iii) Find the threshold frequency and the work function of the cathode in eV.", a: "(i) Slope = ΔV₀/Δν = (1.43 − 0.60)/(2.0 × 10¹⁴) = 4.15 × 10⁻¹⁵ V s.\n(ii) Slope = h/e, so h = 4.15 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.64 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.\n(iii) V₀ = 0 at ν₀: ν₀ = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ − 0.60/(4.15 × 10⁻¹⁵) = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ − 1.45 × 10¹⁴ ≈ 4.55 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Work function φ₀ = hν₀/e = 4.15 × 10⁻¹⁵ × 4.55 × 10¹⁴ ≈ 1.9 eV.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "atoms": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The α-particle scattering experiment led Rutherford to conclude that:", options: ["an atom is a uniform sphere of positive charge", "the positive charge and most of the mass of an atom lie in a tiny central nucleus", "electrons are embedded in a positive sphere", "atoms are indivisible"], answer: 1, explain: "Most α-particles passed straight through but a few were deflected through large angles, showing a small, dense, positively charged nucleus.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The energy of the electron in the ground state of the hydrogen atom is:", options: ["−3.4 eV", "+13.6 eV", "zero", "−13.6 eV"], answer: 3, explain: "E_n = −13.6/n² eV, so for n = 1 the energy is −13.6 eV. The negative sign means the electron is bound.", level: 1 },
      { q: "According to Bohr's postulate, the angular momentum of the electron in a stationary orbit is:", options: ["nh/π", "2πh/n", "nh/2π", "n²h/2π"], answer: 2, explain: "Bohr's quantisation condition is L = mvr = nh/2π, where n = 1, 2, 3, …", level: 1 },
      { q: "The series of hydrogen lines that lies in the visible region is the:", options: ["Balmer series", "Lyman series", "Paschen series", "Pfund series"], answer: 0, explain: "Balmer lines are produced by transitions to n = 2 and lie between about 400 nm and 656 nm. Lyman is in the ultraviolet, Paschen and Pfund in the infrared.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The radius of the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen is about:", options: ["5.3 Å", "0.053 Å", "0.53 Å", "1.06 Å"], answer: 2, explain: "r_n = 0.53 n² Å, so for n = 1, r = 0.53 Å = 5.3 × 10⁻¹¹ m.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The wavelength of the line produced by the transition n = 3 → n = 2 in hydrogen is about (hc = 1240 eV nm):", options: ["486 nm", "656 nm", "122 nm", "1875 nm"], answer: 1, explain: "ΔE = 13.6 (1/4 − 1/9) = 13.6 × 5/36 = 1.89 eV. λ = 1240/1.89 ≈ 656 nm (red line of the Balmer series).", level: 2 },
      { q: "The radius of the third Bohr orbit of hydrogen is:", options: ["1.59 Å", "2.12 Å", "4.77 Å", "1.06 Å"], answer: 2, explain: "r₃ = 0.53 × 3² = 0.53 × 9 = 4.77 Å.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The energy required to remove the electron from a hydrogen atom in the n = 2 state is:", options: ["13.6 eV", "10.2 eV", "6.8 eV", "3.4 eV"], answer: 3, explain: "E₂ = −13.6/4 = −3.4 eV, so 3.4 eV is needed to take the electron to infinity (E = 0).", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In Bohr's model, an electron moving in a stationary orbit does not radiate energy even though it is accelerating.\nReason (R): Bohr postulated that an electron emits radiation only when it makes a transition from one stationary orbit to another.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "This is exactly Bohr's second postulate: no radiation in a stationary orbit, and a photon of energy hν = E_i − E_f only during a transition. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A student views a hydrogen discharge lamp through a diffraction grating and sees only a few sharp, coloured lines on a dark background, not a continuous rainbow.\nThis is because:", options: ["the grating blocks most colours", "the hydrogen atom has discrete energy levels, so only certain photon energies are emitted", "the electrons in the atom move continuously through all energies", "hydrogen emits all wavelengths but the eye can see only a few"], answer: 1, explain: "Electron energies in an atom are quantised. Transitions between levels emit photons of definite energies hν = E_i − E_f, giving a line spectrum.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The ratio of the longest wavelength of the Lyman series to the longest wavelength of the Balmer series of hydrogen is:", options: ["27 : 5", "3 : 4", "5 : 27", "1 : 4"], answer: 2, explain: "Longest Lyman: 1/λ = R(1 − 1/4) = 3R/4. Longest Balmer: 1/λ = R(1/4 − 1/9) = 5R/36. λ_L/λ_B = (5R/36)/(3R/4) = 5/27.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A sample of hydrogen atoms is excited to the n = 4 level. The number of lines of the Balmer series (visible region) that can be emitted is:", options: ["3", "2", "6", "1"], answer: 1, explain: "Balmer lines end at n = 2. From n = 4 the possible transitions are 4 → 2 and 3 → 2 (reached via 4 → 3). That gives 2 lines. (The total number of lines of all series is 6.)", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The radius of the nth Bohr orbit of hydrogen is proportional to n².\nReason (R): The speed of the electron in the nth orbit is proportional to 1/n².",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "r_n = 0.53 n² Å, so A is true. The orbital speed is v_n = 2.2 × 10⁶/n m s⁻¹, proportional to 1/n, not 1/n², so R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A hydrogen atom in its ground state absorbs a photon of energy 12.09 eV and the electron jumps to a higher orbit.\nThe kinetic energy of the electron in the new orbit is:", options: ["12.09 eV", "3.02 eV", "−1.51 eV", "1.51 eV"], answer: 3, explain: "E_n = −13.6 + 12.09 = −1.51 eV, which is n = 3. In a Bohr orbit KE = −E_n, so KE = +1.51 eV.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Bohr's quantisation condition for the angular momentum of the electron in a hydrogen atom.", a: "Of all the possible circular orbits, the electron can move only in those for which its angular momentum is an integral multiple of h/2π: L = mvr = nh/2π, n = 1, 2, 3, …", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the Rydberg formula for the wavelengths of the hydrogen spectrum. State the value of the Rydberg constant.", a: "1/λ = R (1/n₁² − 1/n₂²), where n₂ > n₁, with n₁ fixed for a series.\nRydberg constant R = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two limitations of Rutherford's nuclear model of the atom.", a: "(i) It cannot explain the stability of the atom: an orbiting electron is accelerated, so it should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus.\n(ii) It cannot explain the line spectra of atoms: the radiation from a spiralling electron would be continuous, whereas atoms emit only discrete wavelengths.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the radius and the orbital speed of the electron in the n = 2 orbit of hydrogen. (Use r_n = 0.53 n² Å and v_n = 2.2 × 10⁶/n m s⁻¹)", a: "r₂ = 0.53 × 2² = 2.12 Å = 2.12 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nv₂ = 2.2 × 10⁶ / 2 = 1.1 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A 5 MeV α-particle is fired head-on at a gold nucleus (Z = 79). Find its distance of closest approach. (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻², e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)", a: "At closest approach all the kinetic energy becomes electrical potential energy: K = (1/4πε₀)(2e)(Ze)/r₀, so r₀ = 2Ze²/(4πε₀K).\nK = 5 MeV = 5 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J = 8 × 10⁻¹³ J.\nr₀ = (9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 79 × (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)²)/(8 × 10⁻¹³) = 3.64 × 10⁻²⁶ / 8 × 10⁻¹³ ≈ 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ m.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "An electron in a hydrogen atom makes a transition from n = 4 to n = 2. Find the energy and wavelength of the emitted photon and name the series. (hc = 1240 eV nm)", a: "E = 13.6 (1/2² − 1/4²) = 13.6 × (1/4 − 1/16) = 13.6 × 3/16 = 2.55 eV.\nλ = hc/E = 1240/2.55 ≈ 486 nm.\nThe transition ends at n = 2, so it belongs to the Balmer series (visible, blue-green).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how de Broglie's hypothesis accounts for Bohr's quantisation of angular momentum.", a: "(i) An electron of momentum p has wavelength λ = h/p = h/mv.\n(ii) For a stable orbit the electron wave must form a standing wave, so the circumference holds a whole number of wavelengths: 2πr_n = nλ.\n(iii) Substituting: 2πr_n = nh/mv, so mvr_n = nh/2π, which is Bohr's quantisation condition.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using Bohr's postulates, derive an expression for the radius of the nth orbit and the energy of the electron in the nth orbit of the hydrogen atom, and show that the ground-state energy is −13.6 eV.", a: "(i) The Coulomb force supplies the centripetal force: mv²/r = e²/(4πε₀r²), so mv² = e²/(4πε₀r).\n(ii) Quantisation: mvr = nh/2π, so v = nh/(2πmr).\n(iii) Substituting v: m n²h²/(4π²m²r²) = e²/(4πε₀r), which gives r_n = n²h²ε₀/(πme²) = 0.53 n² Å.\n(iv) KE = ½mv² = e²/(8πε₀r), PE = −e²/(4πε₀r), so E = −e²/(8πε₀r).\n(v) Substituting r_n: E_n = −me⁴/(8ε₀²h²n²). Putting in the constants gives E_n = −13.6/n² eV, so for n = 1, E₁ = −13.6 eV.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Name the series of the hydrogen spectrum with n₁ = 1, 2 and 3 and state the region of the spectrum for each. Calculate (a) the shortest wavelength of the Lyman series, (b) the longest wavelength of the Balmer series, (c) the longest wavelength of the Paschen series. (R = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹)", a: "Lyman (n₁ = 1): ultraviolet. Balmer (n₁ = 2): visible. Paschen (n₁ = 3): infrared.\n(a) Shortest Lyman: n₂ → ∞, 1/λ = R, λ = 1/(1.097 × 10⁷) = 9.12 × 10⁻⁸ m ≈ 91 nm.\n(b) Longest Balmer: n₂ = 3, 1/λ = R(1/4 − 1/9) = R × 5/36 = 1.524 × 10⁶ m⁻¹, so λ ≈ 6.56 × 10⁻⁷ m = 656 nm.\n(c) Longest Paschen: n₂ = 4, 1/λ = R(1/9 − 1/16) = R × 7/144 = 5.33 × 10⁵ m⁻¹, so λ ≈ 1.875 × 10⁻⁶ m = 1875 nm.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The lowest energy levels of the hydrogen atom are E₁ = −13.6 eV, E₂ = −3.4 eV, E₃ = −1.51 eV and E₄ = −0.85 eV. (hc = 1240 eV nm)\n(i) How much energy must a ground-state atom absorb to reach n = 2?\n(ii) Find the wavelength of the photon emitted in the transition n = 3 → n = 1.\n(iii) What is the minimum energy needed to ionise an atom that is in the n = 2 level?", a: "(i) ΔE = E₂ − E₁ = −3.4 − (−13.6) = 10.2 eV.\n(ii) ΔE = E₃ − E₁ = −1.51 + 13.6 = 12.09 eV. λ = 1240/12.09 ≈ 102.6 nm (ultraviolet, Lyman series).\n(iii) Ionisation energy from n = 2 = 0 − E₂ = 3.4 eV.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "nuclei": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The nuclear force between nucleons is:", options: ["long-ranged and depends on charge", "short-ranged, strong and nearly independent of charge", "long-ranged and always repulsive", "the same as the gravitational force"], answer: 1, explain: "Nuclear force acts only over a few femtometres, is much stronger than the Coulomb force and is nearly the same for n–n, n–p and p–p pairs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Isotopes of an element have the same:", options: ["mass number", "number of neutrons", "nucleon number", "atomic number"], answer: 3, explain: "Isotopes have the same Z (same element) but different numbers of neutrons, hence different mass numbers.", level: 1 },
      { q: "1 atomic mass unit (1 u) is equivalent to an energy of:", options: ["9.315 MeV", "93.15 MeV", "931.5 MeV", "9315 MeV"], answer: 2, explain: "Using E = mc², 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg is equivalent to about 931.5 MeV.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The binding energy per nucleon is greatest for nuclei with mass number close to:", options: ["56", "2", "120", "238"], answer: 0, explain: "The curve peaks at about 8.75 MeV near A = 56 (iron), so these are the most stable nuclei.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The density of nuclear matter is:", options: ["proportional to the mass number A", "proportional to A²", "inversely proportional to A", "independent of the mass number"], answer: 3, explain: "The mass is proportional to A and the volume (∝ R³ ∝ A) is also proportional to A, so the density (about 2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg m⁻³) is the same for all nuclei.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The radius of a nucleus of mass number 64 is (R₀ = 1.2 fm):", options: ["7.7 fm", "4.8 fm", "1.2 fm", "3.2 fm"], answer: 1, explain: "R = R₀A^(1/3) = 1.2 × 64^(1/3) = 1.2 × 4 = 4.8 fm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The mass of a deuterium atom is 2.01410 u. Given m_H = 1.00783 u and m_n = 1.00866 u, the binding energy of the deuteron is about:", options: ["1.1 MeV", "4.4 MeV", "2.2 MeV", "22 MeV"], answer: 2, explain: "Mass defect = 1.00783 + 1.00866 − 2.01410 = 0.00239 u. Binding energy = 0.00239 × 931.5 ≈ 2.2 MeV.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The ratio of the radii of nuclei with mass numbers 8 and 125 is:", options: ["2 : 5", "8 : 125", "4 : 25", "1 : 15"], answer: 0, explain: "R ∝ A^(1/3), so R₁/R₂ = (8/125)^(1/3) = 2/5.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The binding energy per nucleon is nearly constant, about 8 MeV, for nuclei with 30 < A < 170.\nReason (R): The nuclear force is short-ranged and saturates, so each nucleon interacts only with its immediate neighbours.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "If every nucleon interacted with all the others, binding energy per nucleon would rise with A. Saturation of the short-range force makes it roughly constant.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A small research reactor produces 3.2 MW of power from the fission of ²³⁵U, each fission releasing about 200 MeV (1 MeV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J).\nThe number of fissions per second is about:", options: ["1 × 10¹⁹", "1 × 10¹⁷", "1.6 × 10⁴", "6.4 × 10¹⁷"], answer: 1, explain: "Energy per fission = 200 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ J. Number per second = 3.2 × 10⁶ / 3.2 × 10⁻¹¹ = 1 × 10¹⁷.", level: 2 },

      { q: "The atomic mass of ⁴₂He is 4.0026 u. With m_H = 1.00783 u and m_n = 1.00866 u, the binding energy per nucleon of helium is about:", options: ["28.3 MeV", "7.07 MeV", "3.54 MeV", "14.2 MeV"], answer: 1, explain: "Δm = 2(1.00783) + 2(1.00866) − 4.0026 = 0.03038 u. E_b = 0.03038 × 931.5 ≈ 28.3 MeV. Per nucleon: 28.3/4 ≈ 7.07 MeV.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The binding energy per nucleon is 1.11 MeV for ²H and 7.07 MeV for ⁴He. The energy released in the fusion ²H + ²H → ⁴He is about:", options: ["28.3 MeV", "4.4 MeV", "23.8 MeV", "32.7 MeV"], answer: 2, explain: "E_b(⁴He) = 4 × 7.07 = 28.28 MeV. E_b(²H) = 2 × 1.11 = 2.22 MeV, so two deuterons have 4.44 MeV. Energy released = 28.28 − 4.44 ≈ 23.8 MeV.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The density of a nucleus depends on its mass number.\nReason (R): The volume of a nucleus is proportional to its mass number.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true: V ∝ R³ ∝ A. Since the mass is also proportional to A, the density is the same for all nuclei, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A coal-fired power plant burns coal that gives 3 × 10⁷ J per kg. A student wants to compare it with the fission of 1 g of ²³⁵U, taking 200 MeV per fission and N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ per mole.\nThe mass of coal that gives the same energy as 1 g of ²³⁵U is about:", options: ["27 kg", "2.7 × 10³ kg", "2.7 × 10⁶ kg", "270 kg"], answer: 1, explain: "Atoms in 1 g = 6.02 × 10²³/235 = 2.56 × 10²¹. Energy = 2.56 × 10²¹ × 200 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J ≈ 8.2 × 10¹⁰ J. Coal needed = 8.2 × 10¹⁰ / 3 × 10⁷ ≈ 2.7 × 10³ kg.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define mass defect of a nucleus.", a: "Mass defect is the difference between the total mass of the separate nucleons and the actual mass of the nucleus: Δm = [Zmₚ + (A − Z)mₙ] − M. It is always positive.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define the atomic mass unit and give its equivalent in MeV.", a: "1 atomic mass unit (1 u) is 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. It is equivalent to 931.5 MeV.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Show that the density of nuclear matter is independent of the mass number. Estimate its value. (R₀ = 1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ m, 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg)", a: "Mass of nucleus ≈ A u = A × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. Volume = (4/3)πR³ = (4/3)π R₀³A.\nDensity = A(1.66 × 10⁻²⁷) / [(4/3)π R₀³A]. A cancels, so the density is independent of A.\nρ = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ / [(4/3)π × (1.2 × 10⁻¹⁵)³] = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ / 7.24 × 10⁻⁴⁵ ≈ 2.3 × 10¹⁷ kg m⁻³.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State any two properties of the nuclear force.", a: "(i) It is a short-range force that is negligible beyond a few femtometres, and is attractive for separations above about 0.8 fm but strongly repulsive at smaller separations.\n(ii) It is much stronger than the Coulomb force and nearly independent of charge: n–n, n–p and p–p forces are about equal.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The atomic mass of ¹⁶₈O is 15.99492 u. Calculate its binding energy and binding energy per nucleon. (m_H = 1.00783 u, m_n = 1.00866 u, 1 u = 931.5 MeV)", a: "Mass of 8 H atoms + 8 neutrons = 8(1.00783) + 8(1.00866) = 8.06264 + 8.06928 = 16.13192 u.\nMass defect Δm = 16.13192 − 15.99492 = 0.13700 u.\nBinding energy = 0.13700 × 931.5 ≈ 127.6 MeV.\nBinding energy per nucleon = 127.6/16 ≈ 7.98 MeV.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the binding energy curve, explain why energy is released both when a heavy nucleus undergoes fission and when light nuclei undergo fusion.", a: "(i) The binding energy per nucleon rises from light nuclei to a maximum of about 8.75 MeV near A = 56, then falls slowly to about 7.6 MeV for A ≈ 238.\n(ii) In fission, a heavy nucleus (about 7.6 MeV per nucleon) splits into two medium nuclei (about 8.5 MeV per nucleon). The products are more tightly bound, so the difference is released as energy.\n(iii) In fusion, light nuclei (for example ²H at about 1.1 MeV per nucleon) combine into a heavier nucleus with a larger binding energy per nucleon, and energy is released in the same way.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Calculate the energy in joules released when 1 g of ²³⁵U undergoes complete fission, taking 200 MeV per fission. (N_A = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹, 1 MeV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ J)", a: "Number of atoms in 1 g = 6.02 × 10²³ / 235 = 2.56 × 10²¹.\nEnergy = 2.56 × 10²¹ × 200 MeV = 5.12 × 10²³ MeV.\nIn joules: 5.12 × 10²³ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ ≈ 8.2 × 10¹⁰ J.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the binding energy per nucleon curve. State its main features and what they tell us about nuclear stability and nuclear force.", a: "The curve plots binding energy per nucleon against mass number A.\n(i) For light nuclei (A < 30) it is low and rises with A, with peaks at ⁴He, ¹²C, ¹⁶O.\n(ii) For 30 < A < 170 it is nearly constant at about 8 MeV, showing that the nuclear force saturates (each nucleon interacts only with its close neighbours).\n(iii) It has a maximum of about 8.75 MeV at A ≈ 56 (iron), so these nuclei are the most stable.\n(iv) For heavy nuclei it falls slowly to about 7.6 MeV at A = 238, because the Coulomb repulsion between many protons reduces binding. Heavy nuclei are less stable.\n(v) A higher binding energy per nucleon means greater stability; moving towards the peak (by fission of heavy nuclei or fusion of light ones) releases energy.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Distinguish between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion. Give one example reaction of each, the energy released, the conditions needed, and one place where each occurs or is used.", a: "(i) Fission: a heavy nucleus splits into two medium nuclei after absorbing a neutron. Example: ²³⁵₉₂U + ¹₀n → ¹⁴⁴₅₆Ba + ⁸⁹₃₆Kr + 3 ¹₀n + energy. About 200 MeV is released per fission.\n(ii) The extra neutrons can cause a chain reaction, which is controlled in a nuclear reactor to produce power.\n(iii) Fusion: two light nuclei combine into a heavier one. Example: ²₁H + ²₁H → ³₂He + ¹₀n + 3.27 MeV.\n(iv) Fusion needs extremely high temperatures (about 10⁷ K or more) so that the nuclei overcome their Coulomb repulsion: thermonuclear fusion. It powers the Sun and stars, where hydrogen is converted to helium.\n(v) Per unit mass, fusion releases more energy than fission, but fission is easier to achieve and control.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The atomic mass of carbon-12 is 12.000 u. A student calculates its nuclear binding energy using m_H = 1.00783 u and m_n = 1.00866 u, with 1 u = 931.5 MeV.\n(i) Find the mass defect of ¹²₆C.\n(ii) Find its binding energy in MeV.\n(iii) Find the binding energy per nucleon and compare it with the maximum value on the curve (about 8.75 MeV).", a: "(i) Mass of 6 H atoms + 6 neutrons = 6(1.00783) + 6(1.00866) = 6.04698 + 6.05196 = 12.09894 u. Mass defect = 12.09894 − 12.000 = 0.09894 u.\n(ii) Binding energy = 0.09894 × 931.5 ≈ 92.2 MeV.\n(iii) Per nucleon = 92.2/12 ≈ 7.68 MeV. This is a little below the maximum of about 8.75 MeV at A = 56, so carbon-12 is stable but less tightly bound than iron.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "semiconductor-electronics": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following is a suitable dopant to make n-type silicon?", options: ["Boron", "Phosphorus", "Indium", "Aluminium"], answer: 1, explain: "Phosphorus is pentavalent: four of its electrons form bonds and the fifth is free, making the crystal n-type. Boron, aluminium and indium are trivalent (p-type dopants).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The majority charge carriers in a p-type semiconductor are:", options: ["electrons", "protons", "donor ions", "holes"], answer: 3, explain: "Trivalent acceptor atoms create vacancies (holes), so n_h ≫ n_e.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The energy gap of silicon is approximately:", options: ["0.71 eV", "5.4 eV", "1.1 eV", "3.0 eV"], answer: 2, explain: "Silicon has E_g ≈ 1.1 eV; germanium has 0.71 eV and diamond (an insulator) about 5.4 eV.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The depletion region of a p-n junction contains:", options: ["immobile ionised donor and acceptor atoms", "only free electrons", "only free holes", "equal numbers of free electrons and holes"], answer: 0, explain: "Diffusing carriers recombine near the junction, leaving behind fixed positive donor ions on the n-side and negative acceptor ions on the p-side, with no free carriers.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When a p-n junction diode is forward biased, the depletion region:", options: ["widens and the barrier height increases", "narrows and the barrier height decreases", "disappears at any voltage", "remains unchanged"], answer: 1, explain: "The external voltage opposes the built-in potential, so the barrier falls and the depletion layer becomes thinner, allowing a large current.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor increases with temperature because:", options: ["the band gap becomes larger", "the atoms vibrate less", "more electron–hole pairs are generated by thermal energy", "impurities are added"], answer: 2, explain: "Higher temperature breaks more covalent bonds, so n_e = n_h = n_i increases and so does the conductivity.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In a silicon crystal n_i = 1.5 × 10¹⁶ m⁻³. After doping, the electron concentration is 4.5 × 10²² m⁻³. The hole concentration is:", options: ["4.5 × 10²² m⁻³", "1.5 × 10¹⁶ m⁻³", "2.25 × 10³² m⁻³", "5 × 10⁹ m⁻³"], answer: 3, explain: "n_e n_h = n_i², so n_h = (1.5 × 10¹⁶)² / (4.5 × 10²²) = 2.25 × 10³² / 4.5 × 10²² = 5 × 10⁹ m⁻³.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The current through a forward-biased diode rises from 10 mA to 20 mA when the voltage across it is raised by 0.1 V. The dynamic resistance of the diode is:", options: ["1 Ω", "10 Ω", "100 Ω", "0.1 Ω"], answer: 1, explain: "r_d = ΔV/ΔI = 0.1 V / 10 mA = 0.1 / 0.01 = 10 Ω.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): At 0 K a pure silicon crystal behaves as an insulator.\nReason (R): At 0 K all the electrons are in the valence band and none is in the conduction band.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "With no thermal energy, no electron can cross the band gap, so there are no free carriers and the crystal cannot conduct. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A student connects a capacitor in parallel with the load of a full-wave rectifier. The oscilloscope trace, which earlier showed a series of pulses reaching zero, now looks almost flat.\nThe capacitor has:", options: ["increased the output frequency to 200 Hz", "reversed the polarity of the output", "converted the DC into AC", "smoothed the pulsating output into a nearly steady DC"], answer: 3, explain: "The capacitor charges to the peak and discharges slowly through the load between pulses, filtering out the ripple.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Two identical silicon diodes (threshold 0.7 V each) are connected in series, forward biased, with a 360 Ω resistor across a 5.0 V battery. The current in the circuit is:", options: ["11.9 mA", "13.9 mA", "10 mA", "7.2 mA"], answer: 2, explain: "Voltage left for the resistor = 5.0 − 2(0.7) = 3.6 V. I = 3.6/360 = 0.010 A = 10 mA.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The longest wavelength of light that can lift an electron across the band gap of silicon (E_g = 1.1 eV) is about (hc = 1240 eV nm):", options: ["564 nm", "1127 nm", "2254 nm", "1.1 nm"], answer: 1, explain: "λ_max = hc/E_g = 1240 / 1.1 ≈ 1127 nm (infrared). Photons of longer wavelength have too little energy.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): An n-type semiconductor carries a net negative charge.\nReason (R): The majority carriers in an n-type semiconductor are electrons.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. But each free electron is matched by a positive donor ion, so the crystal as a whole stays electrically neutral; A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a school lab a full-wave rectifier (two diodes, centre-tapped transformer) is run from the 50 Hz mains and shows 100 Hz pulses on the oscilloscope. One day the display shows 50 Hz pulses with a flat gap between them.\nThe most likely cause is that:", options: ["the supply frequency has dropped to 25 Hz", "both diodes are connected in reverse", "one diode has failed (open), so the circuit works as a half-wave rectifier", "a filter capacitor has been added"], answer: 2, explain: "Each diode handles one half-cycle. If one fails open, only one half-cycle passes: half-wave rectification with output frequency equal to the input (50 Hz). Reversing both diodes would only invert the output polarity.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a hole in a semiconductor?", a: "A hole is the vacancy left in a covalent bond when an electron escapes from it. It behaves as a mobile carrier with an effective positive charge equal to that of an electron in magnitude.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by doping? Name one donor impurity.", a: "Doping is the addition of a small amount (a few parts per million) of a suitable impurity to a pure semiconductor to increase its conductivity. Donor impurities are pentavalent atoms, for example phosphorus, arsenic or antimony.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between an intrinsic and an extrinsic semiconductor.", a: "Intrinsic: a pure semiconductor (such as Si or Ge) in which the number of electrons equals the number of holes, n_e = n_h = n_i; its conductivity is low and depends on temperature.\nExtrinsic: a semiconductor doped with donor or acceptor impurities, so one type of carrier dominates (n-type: n_e ≫ n_h; p-type: n_h ≫ n_e), giving much higher conductivity.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A silicon diode (threshold voltage 0.7 V) is forward biased in series with a 230 Ω resistor and a 3.0 V battery. Find the current in the circuit.", a: "Voltage across the resistor = 3.0 − 0.7 = 2.3 V.\nI = V/R = 2.3/230 = 0.01 A = 10 mA.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how the depletion region and the barrier potential form when a p-n junction is made.", a: "(i) Electrons diffuse from the n-side to the p-side and holes from the p-side to the n-side because of the difference in concentration; this is the diffusion current.\n(ii) The carriers that cross recombine, leaving fixed positive donor ions near the junction on the n-side and fixed negative acceptor ions on the p-side. This region is the depletion layer; it has no free carriers.\n(iii) The ions set up an electric field from n to p, i.e. a barrier potential, which opposes further diffusion. The field drives a drift current of minority carriers; at equilibrium drift and diffusion currents are equal and the net current is zero.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "On the basis of energy bands, distinguish between metals, insulators and semiconductors.", a: "(i) Metals: the conduction band is partly filled or overlaps the valence band, so there is no gap and many free electrons are available; they have very low resistivity.\n(ii) Insulators: the energy gap between the valence and conduction bands is large (E_g > 3 eV; about 5.4 eV for diamond), so practically no electrons reach the conduction band.\n(iii) Semiconductors: the energy gap is small (E_g < 3 eV; 1.1 eV for Si, 0.71 eV for Ge), so at room temperature some electrons cross into the conduction band, leaving holes. At 0 K they behave as insulators.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A pure silicon crystal has an intrinsic carrier concentration of 1.5 × 10¹⁶ m⁻³. It is doped with phosphorus so that the donor concentration is 5 × 10²² m⁻³. Find the electron and hole concentrations and state the type of the semiconductor.", a: "Practically every donor gives one free electron, so n_e ≈ N_D = 5 × 10²² m⁻³.\nUsing n_e n_h = n_i²: n_h = (1.5 × 10¹⁶)² / (5 × 10²²) = 2.25 × 10³² / 5 × 10²² = 4.5 × 10⁹ m⁻³.\nSince n_e ≫ n_h, the semiconductor is n-type (electrons are majority carriers).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "With a circuit diagram, explain the working of a full-wave rectifier using two diodes. State the frequency of the output for a 50 Hz input and the role of a capacitor filter.", a: "Circuit: a centre-tapped transformer has its secondary ends connected to the p-sides of two diodes D₁ and D₂; the n-sides are joined together and connected to one end of the load resistor R_L. The centre tap is the other end of the load.\nWorking: (i) During the first half-cycle, end A of the secondary is positive with respect to the centre tap, so D₁ is forward biased and conducts while D₂ is reverse biased. (ii) During the next half-cycle, end B is positive, so D₂ conducts and D₁ is off. (iii) In both half-cycles the current through R_L flows in the same direction, so the output is unidirectional (pulsating DC).\nFrequency: the output has a pulse in every half-cycle, so its frequency is twice that of the input: 2 × 50 = 100 Hz.\nFilter: a capacitor connected across R_L charges at the peaks and discharges slowly through R_L, smoothing the output into a nearly steady DC.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Draw and explain the I–V characteristics of a p-n junction diode in forward and reverse bias. Mention the threshold voltage, the reverse current and the dynamic resistance.", a: "(i) Forward bias (p to +, n to −): the barrier height and depletion width decrease. Up to the threshold voltage (about 0.7 V for Si, 0.2 V for Ge) the current is very small; beyond it the current rises rapidly (nearly exponentially) for a small increase in voltage.\n(ii) Reverse bias: the barrier height and depletion width increase. Only a very small reverse current (of the order of μA) flows, nearly independent of the voltage, due to minority carriers, until breakdown at a large reverse voltage.\n(iii) The characteristic is not symmetric: the diode conducts well in one direction only, which is why it is used as a rectifier.\n(iv) Dynamic resistance r_d = ΔV/ΔI, the slope reciprocal at the operating point; it is small in forward bias and very large in reverse bias.\n(v) The graph has forward current in the first quadrant, rising sharply after the threshold, and a tiny reverse current in the third quadrant.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A student measures the forward current of a silicon diode. At 0.75 V the current is 5 mA, and at 0.80 V it is 15 mA. When she reverses the battery, the ammeter reads only a few microamperes.\n(i) Find the dynamic resistance of the diode between 0.75 V and 0.80 V.\n(ii) Why is the current so small in reverse bias?\n(iii) What do you expect if the forward voltage is only 0.5 V?", a: "(i) r_d = ΔV/ΔI = (0.80 − 0.75)/(15 − 5) mA = 0.05 / 0.010 = 5 Ω.\n(ii) In reverse bias the barrier and depletion layer widen, so majority carriers cannot cross; only the small flow of minority carriers remains, giving a current of a few μA.\n(iii) 0.5 V is below the threshold voltage (about 0.7 V for Si), so the current would be negligibly small.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
